thank you for the invitation to present  today and i've  i've been asked to present on test  validation in medical laboratories from  an australian perspective um and so  i'll give some context as to where i'm  based and what we do  first so i i  am based at the garvin institute of  medical research which is in sydney uh  it's a beautiful part of the city you  can see from this image  and i've highlighted some of the  the affiliated institutes and hospitals  uh with the garvin institute of medical  research  we have some vincent's hospital the  kinghorn cancer center where i am based  and the garvan institute as well as a  medical school nearby and the victor  chang cardiac research center  so at the garvin institute there are  around 600 scientists  and clinicians but there are more than a  thousand  scientists and clinicians across the  precinct  so it's a very uh rich grounds for  collaboration and for clinical projects  the lab where i'm based and that i'm  director of is  at the kingoran center for clinical  genomics and air lab is the  largest dna sequencing facility in  australia  my research my experience sorry in  research and clinical  testing spans around 20 years as a  cancer researcher i studied  the genetics and epigenetics of  hereditary and sporadic  bowel cancer i was a group leader at  unsw  starting 2009. before that i was in the  uk  and then since uh leaving unsw  i've been in leadership roles in genetic  pathology  first in a private pathology company  known as douglas hunley moya pathology  those of you in the u.s may know this as  sonic healthcare  the headquarters of sonic healthcare is  actually dh  and dhm in sydney and i was the  supervisor of the genetics lab there and  since uh  then i've been laboratory director at  the kinglorn center for clinical  genomics sequencing lab  and in that time i've overseen the  validation and natural accreditation of  multiple single gene  gene panel whole exome and whole genome  services  but that just brings uh my expertise  to highlight why i've been  asked to give this talk and hopefully  shed some light on  what test validation for clinical  testing  is like in australia  my lab is an iso 15189 accredited lab  and also natal accredited for germline  whole genome sequencing in 2016  germ line hole exome sequencing in 2018  and most recently  somatic gene panel testing in 2020  so iso 15189  is a set of australian or international  standards actually  for operating clinical laboratories  and it builds on other standards  such as iso 17025 which is focused on  technical  uh requirements but the iso 15189 is  really focused on clinical safety  so how samples are handled within a  clinical lab  now when it comes to nasa this is an  australian based organization  and so let's go into these in more  detail because it's really important to  understand what these are because they  guide  how we validate tests  in a clinical lab  is the national association of testing  authorities  it provides us administrative support  it provides the assessors to assess a  lab  to determine whether they should and  could offer clinical testing  and it provides the infrastructure  around those  the royal college of pathologists of  australasia they provide professional  advice training and support and work  hand in glove with nata  and npac the national pathology  accreditation advisory council  are a ministerially appointed council  which basically provide  the requirements that a medical  laboratory must follow  in order to perform clinical testing and  natal will assess  a lab based on the requirements that  npac publish  npac have a series of standards  documents separated into tiers which are  used as a  as a a guide to  validating new tests and operating  clinical lab and i've listed them here  so iso 15189 as i mentioned is an  international standard  that outlines the requirements for  quality and competence for medical  laboratories  who perform testing in clinical or  diagnostic settings it's focused on the  clinical and technical validity  patient safety and risk reduction of any  operation within a lab  and as i mentioned there are other  standards that  this builds on such as iso 17025 which  is more focused on technical competence  but in addition the iso 15189 is focused  on management system requirements that  are necessary for it to deliver  testing in a clinically and technically  valid way and what  does iso 15189 consider everything from  pre-analytical  steps such as sampling handling and  transportation  of a sample the validity and  appropriateness  of a method in the lab traceability  of measurements and calibration to  national standards  suitability of those  pieces of equipment for testing the  technical competence of staff which  should be documented and recorded and  continually improved  the quality management system and  also steps that the lab must take to  continually improve what it offers  and some of that involves customer  feedback and making sure that  complaints are addressed  so when it comes to validating a new  test in  an australian medical laboratory any  test that is developed  from scratch or a an off-the-shelf test  that is modified in any significant way  is known as an in-house in vitro  diagnostic medical device or an in-house  ivd the us equivalent would be known as  a  laboratory developed test or an ldt  and again there are requirements that  must be followed  in order to validate a test for clinical  use  as i mentioned npac but also the  therapeutic goods administration  and these are two documents that are  bibles i guess for medical laboratories  who wanted  to validate a new medical test  in terms of managing the lab there are  certain  pragmatic factors that guide  what tests would be validated  and everything from the workflow and the  equipment used  some of those pragmatic factors include  the scalability  and automation of a method  you want to reduce hands-on scientist  time where possible  it may be a test that's amenable or it  may not be amenable to automation  and some of those steps may involve  liquid handling robotics lims  integration  the automation of analysis with  bioinformatics  but also you have to factor in what the  cost of that automation  is you also have to factor in clinical  safety  and whether the test can be operated  with clinical safety in an iso 15189  environment  turnaround time of the test is important  it has to be  guided by the urgency of clinical  management  it makes no sense to have a test that  takes four weeks to perform  but in order to guide clinical  management a clinician may require a  testing three  test result in three days so that's an  important factor as well  and of course the cost cost as in  what's the cogs the cost of goods  for the lab whether  a charge whether the market would be  willing to pay  the cost and also in the australian  context whether there's medi  medicare rebate available that would  cover costs  when it comes to test validation there  are a number of  specific criteria that we need to  address  and this is standard across the world  nothing  too specific here in terms of the  australian context but we would want to  make sure that the test is accurate  is robust precise we need to define the  measurement uncertainty  of any quantitative measurements  and we need to understand the  limitations of the test  and whether there are any potential  causes of false  positives or false negatives and i've  defined  what those terms mean here  so rather than go through a series of  standards and documents that are quite  dry  necessary but quite dry i thought  instead what i would do is go through  my personal  view of test validation in a medical  laboratory  and i'm approaching this in this slide  from the perspective of a researcher  and having worked in a research  environment i'm aware of the way  or the disconnect between research  researchers and research projects  and a a lab that performs medical  testing in a regulated  environment now a researcher  often thinks along these lines  we have an idea does the test have  clinical utility  and along the way you would design a  method  that would be able to measure for  example  mutations in a particular gene you would  then  validate it and then you would have a  test with clinical utility  i think this is the wrong way of going  about  test validation it really doesn't  account for the many factors we need to  take into account  when designing a new diagnostic service  and that's really where we want to get  to a diagnostic service  that has clinical utility that is  clinically accredited and can be  used by clinicians to inform patient  management  that is profitable or at least has its  costs covered is scalable so that if you  receive  50 samples in a year you'll find but if  you receive 2 000 samples in a year you  can cope  that is clinically safe  quick with an appropriate turnaround  time for the clinical question being  asked and is also reliable  so to get to that point yes the test has  to have clinical utility  there also needs to be a business case  and as i go through these slides i'll  define in greater detail what each of  the factors and the the considerations  for each of these  then you design your method you  also need reference samples to test  during test validation which you then  perform and i'll come to the steps there  you need to your laboratory needs to  enroll in quality  assurance programs and this is uh  proficiency tests external proficiency  tests to make sure that your test  is detecting what it should you then  need to resource  the test that may involve training  the scientists to perform it and the  pathologist to interpret it  and then you need to market the test to  make sure that people know  that you have developed this and target  that towards  the most appropriate clinicians  so let me give you a case example in  this case i'm going to describe the  genetic testing  of a gene called ugt1a1  ugt1a1 encodes an enzyme that conjugates  bilirubin in the liver  and if it's not functional then  you end up with the accumulation of  bilirubin in the blood or  hyperbilirubinemia  this is also known as gilbert syndrome  but certain genotypes can also be  predictive  for adverse reactions to a range of  drugs  individual atazanavia  and irinatika indinovir and atazanavira  are used in the treatment of hiv  and irenitycan is used in the treatment  of gastrointestinal cancers  if given these drugs and if you have and  you have  gilbert syndrome you'll have  toxic side effects to to those drugs  so it has clinical utility  but is there a market for it this is  where the business case comes in  you need to understand whether people  will request this test  you need to understand whether anyone  else already offers it  and whether your test is predicted to be  more economical have a superior  diagnostic yield  be more efficient be safer and be  quicker  or you know have a decreased turnaround  time  the method design in this case can be a  simple  pcr and capillary electrophoresis based  method  so that's great because it's quick it's  simple it's easy it's  cheap  now the genotypes that  are diagnostic for gilbert syndrome and  predictive  of adverse reaction to specific drugs  are all located  within a specific region of the ugt1a1  promoter  it's a dinucleotide repeat and  there are only four clinically  actionable variants that we need to  consider  and if you're familiar with the  pharmacogenetic system  of haplotype calling then you'll be  familiar with the star allele system and  they are known as star 1  star 28 star 36 and star 37  so as i mentioned these variants are all  dilute dinucleotide repeat lengths  and they're all within the same location  so a simple pcr capillary  electrophoresis assay  and only four genotypes the number of  samples we would require  is quite small only 20 to 30 with  representative  examples of each of those genotypes  obviously if you were doing gene  sequencing across the entire gene  or a panel of genes or a whole genome  you would require many more reference  genotypes  to be assured that you're detecting the  full  spread of different types of alterations  that you may see  then comes test validation it will  include a  period of test optimization but then you  get on to  testing the specific criteria of  accuracy  robustness measurement uncertainty or  precision  the limitations and then writing  documenting a validation report which  will be submitted to nata  in the australian context  the quality assurance program step is  really important because  it's a regulatory requirement that a  lab involved in medical testing is  enrolled in some  form of proficiency testing and as i  mentioned before this is really  around making sure your test detects  what it should detect and  defining the false positive and false  negative rate  and that's also crucial for  understanding the limitations of the  test  now if there's no  if there's no quality assurance program  available  then the next best option is to  set up a sample exchange with a  laboratory that performs similar  testing and we've had to do that in the  past  as well  then you need to resource the test you  need to  make sure it's scalable cost the  pathologist time the scientist time  standardized where possible and automate  where possible and that may involve the  reporting as well as the wet lab work  you need to make sure that you have the  right number of scientists with  redundancy  redundancy in personnel and equipment  that is  and you need to consider that the other  services within your laboratory  aren't affected by this new service  and that could be in the resourcing  context  but also in the methodological context  is it going to require equipment that  would displace other important equipment  are there contamination risks for  example an amplicon based test next to  a non-amplicon-based test and  other considerations such as those  then you need to train your scientists  in the regulatory environment  you need to make sure that those are  those training exercises are documented  and that there are records for each  member of staff  who will be performing the test  and then finally marketing to your  target audience  with my latest flyers presentations  i often have to go and give  presentations to  to show clinicians and oncologists a new  test that we've developed  uh to explain to them the clinical  utility the test request route  uh and how this compares with other  tests  similar tests that are available  so once you've done all that uh you  submit  a validation report to nata  nafta will then if it's a completely new  scope of service and a scope of  accreditation that you're applying for  then they will visit personally with  technical assessors  and a natal lead assessor and they will  go through with a fine tooth comb  all of the processes that you've set up  that use that that test uses  everything from your quality management  system training records  sops the validation report itself  the way you store samples the way you  return results to  requesting clinicians  and then they deliberate and get back to  you within  a few weeks to potentially a few months  for a complicated  extension of scope  once you've been awarded accreditation  there's then reassessments and  surveillance visits  and these are the surveillance visits  are usually conducted every 12 months  following initial assessment but also  if you have recently changed the way  your lab  operates or your service you may get  a surveillance visit earlier than  expected  and then reassessments are conducted  every 24 months  so there are other important documents  as well  as i mentioned there's the iso 15189 iso  17025 the  mpac tiered documents some of which are  listed here  and there's also published literature uh  describing  standardized frameworks for the  validation and verification of  clinical tests and there's an example  here by  matak setel and with that  that's the end of my presentation and  i'll happily take any questions thank  you for your attention

